WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.551
One of the themes we consistently talk about
here at The Rubin Report is why it is important

00:00:08.551 --> 00:00:11.610
to judge people as individuals and not as
a collective.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:15.930
You as an individual are much more than your
immutable characteristics, be it your skin

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.340
color, religion or sexuality.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:24.010
To judge you on those characteristics is actually
what the essence prejudice really is.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:28.690
Prejudice, of course, means to pre-judge,
so if you look at a Black person, or a Muslim

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:33.600
person, or a gay person, and think you what
they think, or more importantly how they should

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.380
think, based on those characteristics, then
you are actually being prejudiced.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:44.190
Sadly, many of the people who accuse others
of bigotry and racism these days are often

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:49.670
the people who practice this brand of prejudice
the most without even realizing they're doing

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:50.670
so.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:54.290
Just look at some of the things my friends
Larry Elder, Maajid Nawaz, and Ayaan Hirsi

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:58.060
Ali are called by the so-called tolerant side.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:02.390
When you look at a group of people and think
you know what they should think or what's

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:08.110
best for them based on those outward characteristics
and not what's going on in their own mind

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:13.710
you not only lock people into your preconceived
notions of what they are, but stifle the voices

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.820
of the minorities within these minorities.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:23.320
This is why the voices of black conservatives
and gay Muslims are so relevant and interesting.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.979
If you really listen to these minorities within
the minority, you will judge these people

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:32.890
on their thoughts, their logic, and their
reason, instead of simply how they look or

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:33.890
who they love.

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:38.470
In a way, the very existence of these free
thinkers within these minority communities

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:43.880
flips identity politics on its head, showing
the flaws of this post modern intersectional

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.400
way of thinking.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.079
Just see how quickly those who preach identity
politics turn on black or latino people who

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:55.340
have conservative political beliefs or how
easily they dismiss gays or women who talk

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.550
about how they are poorly treated in Islamic
societies.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:04.180
Identity politics only works if you believe
the most important quality about each of us

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:09.759
are the attributes we are born with, thus
cannot change, rather than the ideas which

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:10.899
we come to learn.

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:17.269
I view this twisted ideology as a true existential
danger for the tolerant and liberal, albeit

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:19.840
flawed country that America is.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.540
Martin Luther King Jr's desire for his children
to be judged by the content of their character

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.629
and not the color of their skin is being pushed
back on, but it's being done by the people

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:31.650
who purport to be the tolerant ones.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:35.419
We're continuing our partnership with Learn
Liberty today and joining me is the CEO of

00:02:35.419 --> 00:02:38.819
the Thurgood Marshall College Fund, Johnny
C. Taylor.

00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:44.219
The organization represents nearly 300,000
students who attend the 47 historically black

00:02:44.219 --> 00:02:45.799
colleges in America today.

00:02:45.799 --> 00:02:49.920
I heard Johnny speak at an event in Dallas
a couple months ago and was blown away by

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.739
his passion and commitment to educating young
people and his desire to move past identity

00:02:55.739 --> 00:02:56.919
politics.

00:02:56.919 --> 00:02:59.859
We're going to talk about politics, race and
much more.

00:02:59.859 --> 00:03:05.739
Has the role of the black college changed
as we've become a more fair and just society?

00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:10.170
That's one of many questions I'll ask Johnny,
and I'll only judge him by the content of

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.889
his answers, not the color of his skin...

